      "year": 2025,
      "abstract": "Walter Benjamin, active in the early 20th century the political and social upheavals of industrialization, urbanization, the spread of capitalist consumer culture, the Great Depression, and the rise of Nazism, viewed the crisis of the time not simply as a political phenomenon, but as a crisis of the very concept of \u201cprogress.\u201d He criticized the historicism represented by Ranke for its limitations in simply listing events within a homogeneous and empty timeline, and proposed a qualitative and discontinuous concept of time\u2014\u201cmessianic stasis\u201d\u2014that critically connects specific moments of the past with the present. He argued that Dilthey\u2019s concept of experience remained limited to fragmented impressions and information, failing to integrate them into meaningful experiences, and emphasized the restoration of the storytelling tradition and communal memory. He also criticized the way fascism subordinated power to the \u201caestheticization of politics\u201d through the mobilization of art and imagery to captivate the masses. He warned that blind trust in progressivism, the masses, and the party weakened resistance. \nHe distinguished violence into \u201cmythical violence\u201d that establishes and maintains law and \u201cdivine violence\u201d that destroys law and brings about liberation. He viewed art as a social product and argued that artists, as producers, must participate in social change. In \u201cThe Work of Art in the Age of Mechanical Reproduction\u201d, he analyzed the loss of aura and the rise of exhibition value, and argued that communism, in response to fascism\u2019s glorification of art, must politicize art to transform the masses into creative agents. In short, Benjamin deconstructed the myth of linear progress in the modern era and pursued a view of history that revolutionaryally reconstructed the past and present, as well as the liberating power of art and history against fascism and capitalism.",
